# SYNOPSIS

```swift
enum Profile {
  static var systemBase: UInt64 { get }

  static func deadline(resource: borrowing Handle, capacity: Int64,
                       deadline: Int64, period: Int64) throws(Status)
    -> Handle
  static func apply(_ profile: borrowing Handle, to thread: borrowing Handle)
    throws(Status) -> UInt32
  static func lend(_ profile: borrowing Handle, from object: borrowing Handle)
    throws(Status)
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

A scheduling profile gives a thread a priority or a deadline.
A deadline profile promises `capacity` nanoseconds of CPU time in every `period` nanoseconds, finished within `deadline` nanoseconds of the start of the period.
The values must satisfy 50 microseconds <= `capacity` <= `deadline` <= `period`.
Making one needs the profile system resource, whose base is `systemBase`.

`deadline` makes a deadline profile (`profile_create(2)`).
The kernel admits it or refuses it with a reason.

`apply` applies `profile` to `thread`.
It returns 0, or the reason word with which admission refused a deadline profile (the kernel's `CROI_ADMISSION_*` values).
`lend` makes an interrupt object or a counter lend `profile`'s deadline to each thread it wakes, until that thread next blocks.
Both are `object_set_profile(2)`.

